verdict_notes:
**Overall verdict.** Under a technical lens the evaluation holds up well. Every build, test and launch claim reproduced exactly on this M5 Pro at the current upstream HEAD (772808f1). The build is already up to date and the test suite again reports 9564 passed, 0 failed, 177 ignored. The completeness claims are accurate and, if anything, slightly generous:
- no campaign (the trigger runtime handles 7 events and a small set of actions);
- a placeholder AI;
- no network transport;
- 5 superweapon kinds (Nuke, Chronosphere, ChronoWarp, Dominator, Spy Plane) are parsed but have no launch path.

**Errors found.** They are minor factual slips that do not change any conclusion:
- 16 WGSL files, not 15;
- "Nuke only in test fixtures" is wrong, although "Nuke cannot be launched" is right;
- events 60/61 were omitted from the trigger list;
- "failure screen" is not shown in the log.

**Main correction: HD difficulty is understated.** The renderer is a parity engine throughout:
- CPU-baked palette-index sprite atlases capped at 16384² pages;
- CPU-rasterized R8Uint voxel atlases at 32 facings;
- Nearest sampling everywhere;
- RGB565 byte-level combat lighting;
- no HDR target.

The "2026-level" visuals therefore require a substantial second presentation renderer in Rust/wgpu. That is closer to a rewrite of the presentation layer than to adding override pages. It is still the cleanest possible foundation for that work, because of the enforced sim→render boundary, the existing interpolation and the event outputs (SimFrameOutput, light sources, particles).

**Things I could not verify.** Gameplay, frame rate at Retina resolution, and whether the main menu renders correctly without assets all need retail RA2+YR MIX files, and there are none on this machine.

**Score: 6/10, unchanged.** The build and test health is excellent and reproducible, and the architecture is well suited to adding an HD layer. Step-1 completeness (campaign, real AI, multiplayer, the flagship superweapons) is weak and depends on a single maintainer's high-churn upstream. It is not a web base.

